              "text": "It looks like you might be looking for **Iuka, Mississippi** (spelled with a capital **\"I\"**, which is often misread as a lowercase **\"l\"** / **\"Luka\"** in many sans-serif fonts). \n\nLocated in the far northeast corner of the state, **Iuka** (pronounced *eye-YOO-kuh*) is a historic small city and the county seat of Tishomingo County. It is known for its beautiful natural landscapes, rich history, and small-town Southern charm.\n\nKey highlights and interesting facts about Iuka, Mississippi include:\n\n### 1. Natural Attractions and Geography\n* **Woodall Mountain:** Located just south of Iuka, Woodall Mountain is the highest natural point in Mississippi, sitting at 806 feet (246 meters) above sea level. \n* **The Outdoors:** Surrounded by the scenic foothills of the Tennessee River Valley, Iuka is a gateway to outdoor recreation. It is very close to **Pickwick Lake** and **J.P. Coleman State Park**, making it a popular hub for boating, fishing, and hiking.\n* **Mineral Springs Park:** Located downtown, this park is famous for its natural mineral springs. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries, people traveled from all over to drink the water, which was believed to have healing properties.\n\n### 2. Rich Civil War History\n* **The Battle of Iuka:** Fought on September 19, 1862, this was a fierce and bloody conflict during the American Civil War. Union forces under Major General William S. Rosecrans stopped the advance of Confederate forces led by Major General Sterling Price. Today, visitors can explore the historic battlefield sites and markers.\n\n### 3. \"The Marriage Capital of the South\"\n* Historically, Iuka earned the nickname \"The Marriage Capital of the South\". Because Mississippi did not require a waiting period, blood tests, or strict age verification for marriages, thousands of couples from neighboring Alabama, Tennessee, and beyond traveled to Iuka to tie the knot quickly. \n\n### 4. Historic Landmarks\n* **The Old Tishomingo County Courthouse Museum:** Built in 1870, this Romanesque Revival building is listed on the National Register of Historic Places. Today, it serves as a museum housing Civil War artifacts, local history, and extensive genealogical records. \n* **Downtown Iuka:** The downtown historic district features beautifully restored buildings containing local boutiques, antique shops, and cozy diners.",
